{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"rg -n 'Reviewed:|log.md block|fresh non-author|Session:|Rubric:' project-trajectory/prompts; rg -n 'SR-178' docs/requirements/system-requirements.toml\"","aggregated_output":"project-trajectory/prompts\\adjudicate-amendment.template.md:124:Write your verdict to {verdict}. One line per amended row, in the log.md block format:\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\adjudicate-disposition.template.md:46:Write your verdict to {verdict} in the log.md block format: one `- [BLOCKER|MAJOR|MINOR] <what> -> why -> the concrete change -> @owner` line per finding. Then exactly one machine line:\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\adjudicate-first-approval.template.md:95:Write your verdict to {verdict}. One line per row, in the log.md block format:\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\critique.template.md:34:In coordinator use, Write your verdict to {verdict} in the log.md block format;\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\critique.template.md:39:record at the assigned path. In either case, identify the fresh non-author\nproject-trajectory/prompts\\reviewer.template.md:68:Their specs are the Done-when you map coverage against; nothing here narrows the reading scope below, which stays the diff itself. Review ONLY (1) the diff of the work under review — the exact reading scope is `git diff {trunk}...{head} -- . ':(exclude)docs/iteration' ':(exclude)docs/reviews' ':(exclude)docs/log.md' ':(exclude)docs/log.d' ':(exclude)PROJECT_STATE.html' ':(exclude)docs/open-items.html' ':(exclude)docs/stage'` (three dots against the CURRENT trunk, so a station refresh never re-feeds you trunk's own merged work; session telemetry, verdict records and generated artifacts are excluded because they are records of the process, not the work) — and (2) the requirement surface it must satisfy: AGENTS.md, {process_doc}, the docs/requirements registries, and each work item's own spec file under docs/work/ (plus its `specref` target where that points elsewhere) — the spec of record. Grep the registry rows the diff cites; never read a whole registry file or docs/log.md end to end. If this diff adds or changes requirement rows (SN/SR/TC under docs/requirements), also sweep them against the EXISTING registries — the new rows AND the historical rows they touch — for any contradiction, overlap, or attribute/limit conflict, and raise each as a finding (mark it 'for clarity' at MINOR when it is a wording ambiguity sharper SN/SR/TC language would resolve, not a defect). If the diff under review is a DevStg-Reqs/DevStg-Tests approval (a Status-change commit closing a `[phase]-[g*]` gate), the batch-scoped approval hierarchy is a REQUIRED input: generate it with `{scripts}/trace.py --approve <phase>` and confirm the approved SN->SR->LLR/TC batch — its Requirement/AC, LLR Detail, TC Method/Expected, and any cited rubric — is coherent and complete before endorsing the gate. Flag status.md prose that contradicts a declared policy file's current value as a finding. Do NOT read or trust the implementer's own session notes or self-assessment — a leaked self-assessment collapses review finding-rates several-fold. Run the harness yourself and quote real output; believe nothing you did not observe — but run each instrument ONCE and quote its SUMMARY (`python {scripts}/check.py --jobs 0`: the Check summary block; `python {scripts}/trace.py --strict-integrity`: the final line), never the full advisory stream, which spends hundreds of WARN lines of your context on no finding. Drive the diff's REAL shipped code paths — construct the scenario and run the actual function or flow it changes; primitive probes and plausibility reading are supporting evidence, never the verdict's basis. Before hunting, name the worst failure classes THIS change admits (silent wrong content, fail-open, data loss) and hunt those first, severity-ordered. Hunt within the scope bound {process_doc} §6 \"Review threat model\" sets. Tie severity to reachability: a BLOCKER or MAJOR states a scenario reachable in normal use, and a fail-open, data-loss or security path counts only when a supported workflow or the kit's own routes reach it; a rare or contrived path is MINOR at most, or dismissed under {process_doc} §6 \"Review threat model\". An input outside the spec's declared set is yours to classify, not the set author's: one the change fails closed on is a coverage-extension note, written as prose outside the finding lines with no severity tag, so it never moves the count; one the change fails open on stays a finding at its severity. An APPROVE must mean you tried to break it and failed: map each spec Done-when item to its covering test or call it UNCOVERED, and where the diff adds a regression test for a fixed defect, confirm that test fails on the pre-fix behavior. For each test the diff adds or changes, ask: does it drive the interface the requirement names, not handler source? would it fail on a plausible wrong implementation? is each Done-when item covered? Of each new file and function, ask whether it repeats, exactly or in intent, something that already exists ({process_doc} §3, \"Consolidate, don't duplicate\"), and raise each repeat as a finding. This is an INDEPENDENT parallel review — do not debate another reviewer. Write your verdict to {verdict} in the log.md block format: one `- [BLOCKER|MAJOR|MINOR] <file:line> -> issue -> the concrete change -> @owner` line per finding. When that concrete change ADDS a check, guard, warn, or invariant, the same line must also carry one clause naming why the defect cannot be made UNREPRESENTABLE instead — a stricter type, a deleted code path, or a single owning boundary that validates once and is trusted thereafter (the `antidote` skill's question, \"the smallest change that makes this fix unnecessary\", which you cite, not restate). Warn-first: this binds the remedy's WORDING, not the verdict — no finding is refused, downgraded, or blocked for want of the clause. It is NOT owed by a MINOR `for clarity` wording finding, nor by validation at a genuine trust boundary (where one lies: {process_doc} §3 \"When a guard is owed\"); only by a guard that compensates for a REACHABLE bad state the design could have made unreachable. Naming why construction is unavailable is enough — specifying the structural fix is not your job when it exceeds the diff's scope. Then exactly one machine line:\n541:ONE DECISION PER ROW, and the three clauses fail in different directions, so they separate cleanly: RECORDING an acceptance (this row), REPORTING text that has moved away from the record (SR-178), and REFUSING a record written from anything but the live text (SR-179). A record that exists and rides its approval commit while nothing ever compares live text against it satisfies this row and not SR-178; a comparison that reports drift faithfully against a record a hand edit wrote satisfies SR-178 and not SR-179. Three rows on one (SN-029, B-02) pair is what a need with three separable obligations looks like, and the alternative - one row whose partial pass has no id to report against - is the defect the split rule exists to prevent.\n551:[requirement.SR-178]\n579:rationale = \"\"\"THE ANTI-LAUNDERING half, failing independently of both siblings: a comparison that reports drift faithfully against a record a hand edit wrote is a comparison against a forgery, so SR-178 can pass in full while this row is violated. It is what makes \"the record holds what was actually blessed\" a DECIDABLE property rather than a convention.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
